  "body": "Ordinary quota responses now cool the requested `(account, model)` pair, so a `gpt-5` rate limit leaves `gpt-5-mini` available on that account. Terminal quota codes and authentication failures cool the whole account. Vendor utilization windows and pauses retain their account-wide scope.\n\nCloses #724.\n\n- Add bounded retry rounds, credential caps, cooldown waits and a maximum observed cooldown through environment variables and CLI flags. Defaults retain one-round failover, strict pins and upstream timeouts. Requests are never replayed after output starts.\n- Observe SSE and Codex WebSocket quota errors, retry empty or interrupted streams before output, and report incomplete streams after output starts.\n- Add authenticated, audited runtime strategy changes and pool/account/model cooldown resets. Both endpoints follow management access restrictions and shared authentication lockouts. Resets preserve pauses, usage, credentials and session bindings.\n- Add configurable parent-session affinity, correct programmatic CLI defaults and explicit `false` support in JavaScript/Python bindings. Publish regenerated contracts and a minor-release changelog fragment.\n\nThe branch merges main through its v1.24.0 release, including configurable catalog sources, account policies, admin observability and canonical thinking controls. Published route IDs stay intact: `ModelDefinitions = 102`, with the new `Routing = 103` and `CooldownReset = 104` appended afterward. Compatibility tests check numeric values and ordering. The catalog entry retains its published inventory position, and all 62 operations and 235 HTTP routes remain available. Weighted routing, exact token grants, aliases, prefixes, exclusions, isolated upstream clients and `disable_cooling`/`relay` rules remain supported.\n\nPolicy dispatch shares the retry budget, initial cooldown waits, explicit account attempt limits and response inspection. Regression tests verify active-account attribution and opt-in error captures during failover. Two thinking interactions were reproduced before their fixes: suffix changes bypassed the base model's cooldown, and same-account retry rounds removed encrypted history. Recognized suffixes now share the base model's routing identity; retry preparation strips account-bound history only on an account switch and revalidates the original thinking controls on every retry.\n\nTo reproduce the original issue, return an ordinary quota for `gpt-5` on a selected account, then request `gpt-5-mini` on that account: the sibling was incorrectly excluded. Recorded three-account fixtures cover ordinary/terminal HTTP, SSE and WebSocket quotas, the four disconnect cases, retry limits, strict pins, parent affinity, live controls, authorization and auditing. Injected clocks verify expiration; property tests bound arbitrary reset hints. Commands and fixture provenance are in `experiments/issue-724/README.md` and `tests/fixtures/vendor/openai_responses/README.md`.\n\n```sh\ncargo test --locked --all-features --test accounts_routing_policy_test \\\n  --test pool_failover_test --test management_security_test \\\n  --test network_security_audit_test --test thinking_account_policy_test\n```\n\nThe initial cooldown-wait regression opts into an advancing request clock. It starts each request at an injected Unix timestamp and advances that scoped clock using elapsed Tokio time on every request poll. The cooldown therefore expires during the retry sleep without depending on a tracing callback. This removes the fixture's reproducible missing-event failure while retaining its one-call assertion, five-second deadline and expired-cooldown counterpart. Deliberately removing the initial round handoff fails with two upstream calls. Twenty complete four-thread repetitions of the advancing-clock implementation pass all 1,020 tests; after introducing explicit clock variants, five rebuilt full-suite repetitions with 32 threads pass another 255. Reusable mutation and bounded parallel probes are in `experiments/issue-724/`. Default-off retry diagnostics explain why a further round is unavailable.\n\nComplete local validation of `ac061bf` passes 971 integration/bin tests across 128 suites, 15 doctests, examples and all 2,199 distinct library tests across sixteen partitions. The local library inventory exactly matches normal Linux CI and retains all 2,183 previously enabled tests. The largest library child RSS is 2,255,348 KiB, below the unchanged 2,350 MiB guard. Exact current-binary contracts/bindings, published compatibility, Node/Bun/Python (ten tests each), TypeScript, strict production/integration/example/benchmark Clippy and repository policy checks pass. The existing ignored soak is exercised by the separate passing Linux/macOS soak workflow.\n\nThe final-commit instrumented report covers 80,590 of 92,498 lines (87.13%). The unchanged checker accepts the committed measured baseline of 87.129419% without altering the file; all eight checker tests pass. Coverage floor, ratchet and baseline-review gates pass with no exception. All seven final-commit workflows pass: [CI/CD](https://github.com/link-assistant/router/actions/runs/38015862000), [downstream operations and contracts](https://github.com/link-assistant/router/actions/runs/38015861997), [Docker runtime](https://github.com/link-assistant/router/actions/runs/38015861987), [real-client capture](https://github.com/link-assistant/router/actions/runs/38015861985), [Linux/macOS soak](https://github.com/link-assistant/router/actions/runs/38015862017), [benchmarks](https://github.com/link-assistant/router/actions/runs/38015862028) and [fuzz](https://github.com/link-assistant/router/actions/runs/38015862021). CI/CD includes passing Linux/macOS/Windows tests, strict lint, coverage and the release package build.\n\nThe two CI image builders use Docker's documented `mirror.gcr.io` registry-cache configuration after reproducing Docker Hub's unauthenticated pull limit. The finite manifest probe verifies all four cached hashes against the existing Dockerfile pins. Both image workflows pass on the merged source, including shipped commands, DNS/relay checks and tunnel smoke assertions. Image pins and release-publishing behavior remain intact.\n\nRust API compatibility: the defaultable `AccountRouterOptions`, `PoolPolicy`, `PoolArgs` and `RoutingContext` structs have new settings. Complete downstream struct literals need the added fields or `..Default::default()`. This minor-release source-compatibility exception is documented in `docs/integration/compatibility.md`; the field-addition lint remains enabled at the minor level and other checks remain in place. Published enum numeric values and ordering are preserved.\n",
